      "19348": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "text": "## Summary\n\nWith this patch, a user macro whose expansion ends by calling itself, as in `\\newcommand{\\foo}{\\foo}\\foo` (or two macros calling each other last), stops `tools::parse_Rd()` with the usual \"macros nested too deeply: infinite recursion?\" error instead of hanging it. Fixes a reproducible hang on malformed Rd input, found by fuzzing.\n\n## Details\n\n`xxgetc()` in `src/library/tools/src/gramRd.y` counts nested expansions in `macrolevel` and stops past 1000. The lexer, however, reads past an expansion's `END_MACRO` marker while it scans a macro name at the end of that expansion, so a recursive call in that position starts only after the previous expansion has ended and `macrolevel` never exceeds 1. Recursion followed by more text (`\\newcommand{\\foo}{\\foo x}`) is caught already. Tested at r90940; R 4.6.1 behaves the same.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `rd-macro-tail-recursion-repro.R`; the three tail-recursive cases hang (killed after 5 s), while the control stops with the error.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `rd-macro-tail-recursion.diff`; it also counts consecutive expansions that consume no input and raises the same error past the same limit, with a test in `tests/reg-fuzz.R`. One macro call that fans out into more than 1000 expansions now stops too. Related: bug 19346.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/517, where CI results can be seen.",
